id: movement
name: Movement
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
movement: true
usageLimit: 2/round
exploitable: If you leave the Melee Range of an Opponent.
resource: rapid
resourceMax: 1
resourceLabel: Rapid Movement
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Cross the battlefield. Normal Speed for nothing, or Boosted Speed for 3(T);
  leaving somebody's Melee Range hands them an Exploit.
text: >
  Movement Maneuver [2/Round]: Getting from point A to point B as fast as
  possible.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: If you leave the Melee Range of an Opponent.
  –Effect: Move a number of Squares up to your Normal Speed. You may increase the
  KP Cost of this Maneuver to 3(T) to instead move up to your Boosted Speed.

  * Rapid Movement: When using the Movement Maneuver, you can increase the Ki
    Point Cost by 2(T) to use Rapid Movement. If you do, increase your Strike
    Rolls by 1(T) until the end of your turn and increase your Dodge Roll against
    an Exploit Maneuver provoked by this instance of Movement by 1(T).
  * Exploit on Movement: If you leave the Melee Range of an Opponent when using
    this Maneuver, you provoke the Exploit Maneuver from that Opponent. If an
    Attacking Maneuver used through the Exploit Maneuver deals Damage when used in
    response to this Maneuver, you do not move any additional Squares (you still
    move any number of Squares you would until the Exploit Maneuver was triggered
    and the Action Cost is still spent). For example, if you moved into an
    Opponent’s Melee Range and then continued out of their Melee Range for your
    4th Square of movement as part of an 8 Square movement, they may trigger the
    Exploit Maneuver. If that Exploit Maneuver inflicts Damage, you move the
    initial 4 Squares, but you do not continue your intended 8 Square movement.

# No target, because the target is a Square. How far you may go is a rule and the
# card says it; where you end up is yours to make on the map, as every movement
# here is.

---

# The Strike bonus Rapid Movement buys. "Increase your Strike Rolls by 1(T) until
# the end of your turn" - held as a Resource so the clock can take it away, which
# is the same shape Combat Recovery uses for the Defense Value it costs.
#
# Granted from the code rather than from here, because what grants it is a choice
# made when the Maneuver is used and a script has no way to be told which options
# were taken. The rule itself lives here, where a reader looks for it.
[passive]
if (rapid.stacks > 0) {
    strike += 1(T);
}

# --- What is not built, and why ---------------------------------------------------
#
# Rapid Movement is built, both halves. The Strike bonus is the passive above; the
# Dodge bonus is against "an Exploit Maneuver provoked by this instance of
# Movement", and this card is that instance - an offer carries which card opened
# the door, the attack it becomes keeps it, and the Dodge reads it back. A
# character who moves twice and pays for Rapid Movement once gets it on the one
# they paid for.
#
# Exploit on Movement's second half: "you do not move any additional Squares."
# This system moves nobody - where a Character stands is the table's, and the
# Squares are what the rules turn into arithmetic. So the offer goes out, the card
# says what provoked it in the rulebook's own words, and whether the Damage landed
# in time to stop the rest of the movement is a thing the players say out loud.
# The example in the entry is the ruling, and it is on the card for them to read.
